{"kind":"artifact","text":"Nothing is inherited. Exact clean pushed head 5bc84ca2f3dbfd57258450ce76f32ed7c37b6922 on request/repo-remote-link; tree 24f3ee86, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's round-six child f42f5e35 and my promise 8bdb5b67.\n\nTHE CONTRACT IS NOW A SENTENCE RATHER THAN A RULE. command.Env is a clone of five stated entries and nothing else: HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME at os.DevNull, GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL at os.DevNull. The allowlist, the case-folding comparison, the admission predicate and the duplicate-name reasoning are deleted, because an allowlist with nothing in it should not keep the apparatus of admission.\n\nPATH WENT, AND THE REASON IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A JUDGEMENT. All five commands complete with no environment at all, and exec.Command resolves git to an absolute path against the parent before any child environment exists — I verified both myself. So forwarding PATH bought nothing and left inherited execution influence. The parent's choice of which binary is named git is a deployment trust boundary this constructor does not close and cannot; that is now stated as its own thing rather than folded into the contract.\n\nONE SURVIVOR OF THE DELETION, named rather than smuggled. slices.Clone stays, and it is not an admission rule: it stops a caller who edits one command's Env from rewriting what the next command runs under. It has its own test, which fails when the clone becomes the shared package slice.\n\nTHE REAL TRUST SCOPE, measured rather than described. Not \"the repository's own configuration file\", which was one exercised case and which I published as though it were the boundary. It is repository-local configuration, worktree configuration at GIT_DIR/config.worktree where extensions.worktreeConfig is set, and include.path and includeIf targets absolute or relative. core.fsmonitor was observed executing during an ordinary git status, under exactly the stated environment, from all five of those placements.\n\nA COVERAGE HOLE FOUND WHILE CLOSING THE BLOCKERS, and it is the interesting one. Deleting --local from the remote read passed the entire suite before this head. Closing the environment had made an older guard untestable: with no outer scope reachable, nothing exercised the flag that bounds which scope is read. The new test fails when --local goes. A security fix quietly disarming a neighbouring test is worth knowing about beyond this row.\n\n--no-includes IS SHIPPED AND IS NOT LOAD-BEARING, and I would rather say so than let a flag imply a guard. I verified it: --local already declines includes, --local --includes brings the value back, and --no-includes matches the default exactly. So the flag pins a documented Git default rather than changing behaviour, and no mutation can distinguish it while that default holds. Claiming mutation-sensitivity it cannot have would be the false-witness problem this lane has been about.\n\nA USER-FACING CONSEQUENCE OF THAT, which is a limitation rather than a defect. A remote configured through an include is not read, so the browser shows no link for it. I found this while checking the flag rather than by design, and it belongs in the record because someone will hit it and wonder.\n\nTHE PLATFORM CONTRACT IS NARROWED, per planner's ruling. The closed-environment property is stated for Unix, which is where it is exercised. Windows is named as outside that contract rather than as a caveat inside it: the package builds for it, nothing here has been run on it, and the property is false there because Go's os/exec appends SYSTEMROOT from this process whenever the child environment carries no name folding to it. I verified addCriticalEnv in the Go source rather than from memory. No Windows regression was written, deliberately — a test that has never executed is not evidence, and shipping one beside the claim would give the claim a false witness.\n\nTHE CASE-VARIED TEST WAS REBUILT RATHER THAN DELETED. It tested a folding rule that existed only because the allowlist admitted a name; with nothing admitted there is no case to compare. It now asserts the stronger claim the empty set makes available, one layer further out: it sets every vector including case-varied spellings into the real process environment, drives the constructor all five reads go through, and requires exactly the five stated entries and nothing else.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE. Appending os.Environ() to the stated set fires all six vector regressions and both closed-world tests. Dropping any single stated pin fails its own vector. Sharing the package slice instead of cloning fails the isolation test. Dropping --local now fails, where before it did not. The citation exit-code rule still fails its own test when made fail-open. Dropping --no-includes fails nothing, which is stated rather than hidden.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 241.071s, internal/app 167.353s, internal/kernel 162.402s, internal/docset 106.451s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 93.596s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, GOOS=windows go build ./internal/app/ and git diff --check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux — this ran on darwin/arm64 with git 2.50.1.","body":{"commit":"5bc84ca2f3dbfd57258450ce76f32ed7c37b6922","path":"internal/app"}}